re two  things that I could talk  today why Americans should be more  bilingual or how we become it now given  that we're in an academic setting I'm  gonna assume that many of you have  already heard about the benefits of  bilingualism how bilingualism has been  linked to improved learning outcomes  enhance cognitive abilities more  financial opportunity the development of  empathy and interpretive skills many of  you might know that bilingualism has  been linked to delays and manifestations  of aging like dementia and Alzheimer's  given that this is Stanford after all  some of you may even know that in 2016  the American Academy of Arts and  Sciences released a report that said  that fluency in English is no longer  sufficient to meet the needs of America  in a shrinking world nor the needs of  individual citizens who will interact  with other peoples and cultures more  than at any other time in human history  the overwhelming recommendation of the  Commission was that Americans learn to  value language education the same way we  value education in math in English as a  persistent national need so instead of  talking about why America should become  more bilingual I want to devote the rest  of my time to talking about how how do  we go from our current state where 80%  of Americans are monolingual and start  inching towards becoming more of a  bilingual society I'm gonna suggest we  can take three immediate actions to move  us in that direction first businesses  need to recognize the incredible  opportunity in the space when my husband  and I decided to speak Spanish at home  we were shocked at how few resources we  had to support our decision things like  an alphabet puzzle  where the letter correspondent to the  word in Spanish or books that had a rich  depth of vocabulary we couldn't find  them we ended up having to write our own  books and create our own resources to  support our decision this is a huge  business opportunity we're one of  millions of American parents that are no  longer okay raising a monolingual  generation American parents are looking  for ways to fold language learning into  their family lives I am myself from  starting a business in this space  where we provide in-home support and  guidance for parents and a platform that  connects individuals with language  fluency to language learners in their  communities I'll never forget last  summer after one such community session  a mom coming up to me with tears in her  eyes she said Ana we've chosen to speak  Spanish at home but I've been worried  for years that my daughter would just  associate the language narrowly with us  with our family life that my daughter  wouldn't see its larger place in society  getting to come to this outdoor session  helps my daughter see its larger place  of belonging her words meant a lot to me  because that was the goal of what we  were doing American businesses have an  incredible opportunity to create space  for other languages to belong in our  society and culture my second  recommendation is that American adults  take up a practice of lifelong language  learning now I know what you're thinking  I'm just bad at languages most American  adults feel that way but the truth is  American language education is broken  languages shouldn't be learned in  classrooms through reading and writing  languages need to be lived languages  should be acquired through hearing and  speaking so it's not that you're bad at  languages you likely just haven't had a  fair shot at it  the second think thing I bet many of you  are thinking is what my husband said I'm  too old I'm an adult  don't studies show that if you don't  pick it up as a kid you have no chance  at it  the truth is that learning a language  early in life gives you the benefit of  speaking the language in native  proficiency which simply means you won't  have an accent but there's nothing wrong  with accents accents are beautiful  accents are our proof that it's never  too late to learn another language so  let's remove the stigma around accents  in the United States so that more  American adults are willing to engage in  language education  I learned Arabic as an adult and though  I can't tell you was easy I can tell you  it was incredibly enriching and  something that continues to that I  continue to be grateful for to this day  my third  recommendation is that American parents  or future parents make space in your  homes for family language at learning do  it together kids look at parents for  cues and one of the best pieces of  parenting advice I received is that your  kids will do what they see you do not  what you tell them to do make space in  your homes for language learning devote  an hour a week to speaking to one  another in that target language maybe  your kids in an immersion program bridge  that learning back home maybe your kids  in middle school  surpri and learning a new language  Surprise them with a phrase that your  pick that you're learning you are or can  be your kids safe space do language  learning together it'll make a really  meaningful impact for your kids and our  future generations is it possible for  the United States to become more  bilingual and is it worth the effort my  husband speaks really good Spanish today  and he has this renewed relationship  with his grandparents that's priceless  my son is turning five this month which  I can hardly believe and the other day  he ran up to me and said mommy mommy  let's sing the Arabic number song and he  kicked us off what had this meant that  let our ball humza set sabatham any  epistle  he loves that song and I love that  teaching it to him has led to the  steeper interest he wants to know how  other things are said in Arabic he's  fluent in English in Spanish he's  learning sign language in school and  perhaps most importantly he truly  believes he can learn any language it is  possible to raise a generation of  Americans that embraced bilingualism and  trust me  it's so so worth it thank you  [Applause]  you

